This is a cool architecture — essentially a multi-instrument "forensic DAW" where signals replace sound. Let me sketch out how I'd approach building the first instrument (WiFi scanner) and the surrounding framework it'll plug into. I'll keep this as a design doc rather than jumping straight to code, since the architectural decisions upfront will determine whether instruments 2, 3, 4... slot in cleanly later.
Core architectural concept

Think of it as three layers:

1. Instrument layer — each instrument is an independent module that produces timestamped events. WiFi is first, but the interface needs to be generic: IInstrument produces Event { timestamp_ms, channel_id, magnitude, metadata }. Future instruments (Bluetooth, process spawns, network packets, USB, file I/O, whatever) implement the same interface.

2. Timing/bus layer — a single master clock synchronized to NTP, and an event bus all instruments publish to. This is the "everything runs in unison" guarantee. On Windows, QueryPerformanceCounter gives sub-microsecond resolution; you anchor it to NTP once at startup (and periodically re-anchor) so every instrument's events land on the same absolute timeline.

3. Visualization layer — the main waveform canvas (all toggled instruments overlaid) plus per-instrument panels. This needs GPU acceleration the moment you have more than a few hundred points on screen.
Tech stack recommendation

For Windows with this kind of real-time visualization load, I'd suggest C# with WPF or Avalonia, plus SkiaSharp for the waveform rendering. Reasons:

    C# has excellent Windows APIs (Native WiFi API via P/Invoke for the scanning) and great async support for the event loop
    SkiaSharp handles thousands of points at 60fps without breaking a sweat
    Avalonia if you ever want cross-platform; WPF if you're Windows-forever

Alternative: Rust + egui if you want maximum performance and don't mind steeper development. Python + PyQt6 + pyqtgraph is fastest to prototype but will struggle once you have many instruments running simultaneously.
The WiFi instrument specifically

Here's the event flow I'd build:

Windows exposes WiFi scanning through the Native WiFi API (wlanapi.dll). The key functions are WlanOpenHandle, WlanScan (triggers a scan), and WlanGetNetworkBssList (gets the results including hidden SSIDs, BSSIDs, RSSI in dBm, channel, etc.). A scan cycle takes ~4 seconds on most adapters — that's your sampling rate ceiling for this instrument.

For each scan tick:

    Timestamp the scan completion against the master clock (ms precision, NTP-anchored)
    For every BSSID returned with RSSI ≥ −89 dBm, emit an event
    Append to that BSSID's log file: timestamp_ms, dBm, delta_from_previous
    Push to the event bus for the visualizer

Per-SSID file naming: use BSSID (MAC) not SSID as the key, since SSIDs can collide and hidden ones are blank. Something like logs/wifi/AA-BB-CC-DD-EE-FF.jsonl with one JSON event per line.
One thing to flag about the dBm threshold

You said "above −89 dBm" — just confirming that's what you meant, since dBm is negative and "above −89" means stronger signals (−70, −50, etc.), filtering out the weakest ones. −89 is a reasonable noise floor cutoff. If you meant "anything the card can hear," you'd want no threshold. Let me know which.
