Polyglot architecture the prototype is pointing at:

The C++ core is the master clock authority and timing-critical probe host. It owns QueryPerformanceCounter, does the SNTP query directly in-app against pool.ntp.org (or a configurable server), maintains a drift estimate, and timestamps every event in nanoseconds. It exposes a ZeroMQ PUB socket on tcp://127.0.0.1:5556 with FlatBuffer-encoded events. Probes that need raw OS access or tight latency live here — Npcap for packet inspection later, UBLOX serial, Geiger GPIO.

The C# shell is what you're looking at conceptually — WPF window, SkiaSharp canvases for the score and every instrument panel, SUB socket consuming the bus. It also hosts the easier Windows instruments: Native WiFi API via P/Invoke (wlanapi.dll), Bluetooth LE via Windows.Devices.Bluetooth, audio via WASAPI. These C# probes publish to the same bus the C++ core runs.

Python is a sibling process, another SUB on the bus, running the heuristics engine. NLP, anomaly detection, cross-instrument correlation all live here. It re-publishes derived events (instrument_id = 0xFF = heuristic meta-event) back onto the bus so they render on the master score as their own trace.

What's real vs. simulated in this file. Real: the layout, typography, color system, render loop, hover behavior, tooltip structure, delta coloring, envelope-band math, NTP-style clock display. Simulated: the scan events themselves are a random-walk mock with 12 fake APs, firing every 500ms so the visualization has something to chew on. Replacing this with live data is a 20-line change — drop in a WebSocket or local HTTP polling loop pointing at the C# probe, and the emitScanTick() function becomes onEventReceived().

