What the real implementation already has sitting on disk, ready to wire:

The FlatBuffer schema (schema/signal_event.fbs) already has payload tables defined for all eight instruments plus a Heuristic meta-event — so the wire format is locked and you won't have to retrofit anything when you start building GPS, UBLOX, Geiger etc. The C++ master clock does real SNTP against pool.ntp.org / Cloudflare / Google, hits < 1ms drift (Forensic-grade) when conditions are good, and stamps every event with both monotonic ns and Unix-epoch ns. The C# WiFi scanner uses the actual wlanapi.dll via P/Invoke and writes per-BSSID JSONL logs (logs/wifi/AA-BB-CC-DD-EE-FF.jsonl) with header line + append-only event rows containing timestamp, dBm, delta, clock quality, drift. The WPF shell, the ZMQ sink, the bus consumer, and the Python heuristics engine are all written.

What you need locally to actually run it: Windows 10/11 (for Native WiFi API), .NET 8, Visual Studio / dotnet build for the C# shell, Python 3.10+ with pyzmq+flatbuffers for the heuristics sibling, and flatc to generate language bindings from the schema once. When you're set up, the port to move from the HTML prototype to the WPF XAML I wrote earlier is mainly porting the canvas drawing calls across to SkiaSharp's SKCanvas — which is what that code already does; it just needs the FL-style row layout applied to the XAML.

